:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "29 July 2024"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2173"} "Grand Palais Éphémère, Paris, France"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/2006884"} "30 from 30 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The favorite was " [:a {:href "/athletes/153199"} "Hidayət Heydərov"] " of Azerbaijan, who was the world’s #1 ranked judoka in this class. Heydərov won the 2024 World Championship after taking bronze in the event in 2018, 2019, and 2022. He was European Champion in 2017 and 2022-2024, and had won seven titles at IJF Grand Slams from 2019-2024. The defending gold medalist was Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/134777"} "Shohei Ono"] ", but he had retired."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Heydərov won gold but not without an epic battle in the final, defeating " [:a {:href "/athletes/153253"} "Joan-Benjamin Gaba"] " (FRA) after 9:24 (more than five minutes of Golden Score!) by an " [:em {} "ippon"] " with " [:em {} "ko-soto-gake"] ". In the first bronze medal match, Moldovan " [:a {:href "/athletes/153318"} "Adil Osmanov"] " defeated Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/144182"} "Manuel Lombardo"] " (ITA) by " [:em {} "ippon"] " with " [:em {} "o-ouchi-gari"] ", after 2:19. Osmanov was born in Russia to an Azerbaijani father and a Moldovan mother, but moved to Moldova in 2017. In the second bronze medal match, " [:a {:href "/athletes/153295"} "Soichi Hashimoto"] " (JPN), competing in his first Olympics at the age of 32, defeated Kosovan " [:a {:href "/athletes/145057"} "Akil Gjakova"] " 1-0 by " [:em {} "waza-ari"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Hashimoto had five World Championship medals in this class – gold in 2017, silvers in 2018 and 2022, and bronzes in 2021 and 2023  – but he was not always chosen for international teams because of the presence of Shohei Ono. Gjakova’s sister, " [:a {:href "/athletes/130985"} "Nora"] 